<p>
I liked the reminder to organize events based on what <b>you</b> want, tuning in to what you need and what kind of life you want to have. I like learning about Emacs, so <a href="https://emacsconf.org/organizers-notebook/">EmacsConf</a> is right up my alley, and the fact that I get to tinker with code and processes is actually a large part of the fun. =)
</p>

<p>
I thought it was interesting that Happy Town is focused on building a
collective of event hosts. Sometimes it's hard to find a venue
(especially for free or low-cost non-commercial events), and it sounds
like they have a few places that are happy to host small events. The
next challenge is knowing that at least one other person is going to
show up. That's one of the nice things about developing a community of
hosts and regular participants who can show up for each other.
</p>

<p>
The thing Adam said about asset-based community development is also
interesting. Many people pitching in a little bit of money each can
often work out better than relying on one or two big grants or
sponsorships, thanks to more of a sense of shared ownership. I tend to
shy away from soliciting or managing donations. It gets complicated
when transactions come into play, and I like keeping it gift-based. I prefer
to offer whatever I'm comfortable funding by myself, and my attention
is too limited to scale up much even if other people give money.
People giving time, though&hellip; That's awesome.
</p>

<p>
I think I'm not actually looking for that many new things to add to my
life at the moment, social-wise. The kiddo still wants to spend time
with me, so I want to prioritize her since this time is so fleeting. I
do a bit of volunteering, but it tends to either be things I can do
mostly asynchronously, or things that she's also interested in and
that she can contribute to, or low-commitment things that I can do or
not do. I also tend to focus on opportunities to help out rather than
hang out: we've cooked, we've sorted donated stuffed toys, we've
picked up trash, we've delivered groceries and prepared meals.
</p>

<p>
I find the repetitive scripts and busy-ness of making hot chocolate
for people at protests relaxing, too. ("Would you like dark chocolate,
milk chocolate, or vegan? Half cup or full cup? Marshmallows, candy
canes, both, neither?") I'm glad other people are figuring out what
works well for them and building communities along the way.
</p>

